JAVA OOPs - INTERVIEW NOTES
============================

1. CLASS & OBJECT
-----------------
Class = Blueprint/template.
Object = Real instance of a class.

Example:
class Student {
    String name;
}

Student s = new Student();

Remember:
Class = Blueprint
Object = Real thing


2. CONSTRUCTOR
--------------
Constructor = Used to initialize an object.

Rules:
- Same name as class
- No return type
- Called automatically when object is created

Example:
class Student {
    Student() {
        System.out.println("Created");
    }
}

Student s = new Student();

Remember:
Constructor = Object initialization


3. INHERITANCE
--------------
Inheritance = Child class gets properties/methods from parent.

Keyword: extends

Example:
class Animal {
    void eat() {
        System.out.println("Eating");
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
}

Remember:
Parent -> Child
IS-A relationship


4. POLYMORPHISM
---------------
Polymorphism = One thing, many forms.

2 types:
1. Compile-time -> Overloading
2. Runtime -> Overriding

Remember:
Poly = Many
Morph = Forms


5. ENCAPSULATION
----------------
Encapsulation = Hiding data + controlling access.

Usually:
private variable + getter/setter

Example:
class Student {
    private int age;

    public void setAge(int age) {
        this.age = age;
    }

    public int getAge() {
        return age;
    }
}

Remember:
Encapsulation = Data hiding


6. ABSTRACTION
--------------
Abstraction = Hide implementation details,
show only important functionality.

Achieved using:
- abstract class
- interface

Example:
abstract class Animal {
    abstract void sound();
}

Remember:
Abstraction = WHAT to do
Hide = HOW it is done


7. INTERFACE
------------
Interface = Contract/rules for a class.

Keyword:
implements

Example:
interface Animal {
    void sound();
}

class Dog implements Animal {
    public void sound() {
        System.out.println("Bark");
    }
}

Remember:
Interface = Contract


8. OVERLOADING
--------------
Same method name + different parameters.

Example:
class Calculator {

    int add(int a, int b) {
        return a + b;
    }

    int add(int a, int b, int c) {
        return a + b + c;
    }
}

Remember:
Overloading = Same name + Different parameters
Compile-time polymorphism


9. OVERRIDING
-------------
Child class gives its own implementation
of parent method.

Example:
class Animal {
    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Animal sound");
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    @Override
    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Bark");
    }
}

Remember:
Overriding = Parent method changed by Child
Runtime polymorphism


10. OVERLOADING vs OVERRIDING
-----------------------------

OVERLOADING:
- Same method name
- Different parameters
- Usually same class
- Compile-time

OVERRIDING:
- Parent + Child
- Same method signature
- Child changes parent method
- Runtime


⭐ QUICK REVISION
=================

Class       -> Blueprint
Object      -> Real instance
Constructor -> Initialize object
Inheritance -> Parent -> Child
Polymorphism-> Many forms
Encapsulation-> Data hiding
Abstraction -> Hide implementation
Interface   -> Contract
Overloading -> Same name, different parameters
Overriding  -> Child changes parent method


⭐ INTERVIEW ONE-LINERS
======================

Q: What is OOP?
A: OOP is a programming approach based on objects and classes.

Q: What is a class?
A: A class is a blueprint for creating objects.

Q: What is an object?
A: An object is an instance of a class.

Q: What is inheritance?
A: Inheritance allows a child class to reuse parent class properties and methods.

Q: What is polymorphism?
A: Polymorphism means one thing can have many forms.

Q: What is encapsulation?
A: Encapsulation means hiding data and providing controlled access.

Q: What is abstraction?
A: Abstraction means hiding implementation details and showing only necessary functionality.

Q: What is an interface?
A: An interface is a contract that a class must follow.

Q: What is overloading?
A: Same method name with different parameters.

Q: What is overriding?
A: Child class provides its own implementation of a parent method.